1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,636
This is a short tour of Managed Copilot.

2
00:00:03,936 --> 00:00:12,284
It lets you run GitHub Copilot agents inside environments you own: on your own laptop, or in your own Azure subscription.

3
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:18,687
Apps can already build in Copilot agents with the GitHub Copilot SDK.

4
00:00:18,987 --> 00:00:25,994
Managed Copilot keeps that same SDK, unmodified, and changes only where the agents run.

5
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:31,188
Your code still creates a normal Copilot client and session.

6
00:00:31,488 --> 00:00:38,281
Two setup lines connect it to your platform, which decides where each agent runs and what it can reach.

7
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:45,875
You describe your environments once, in an Aspire AppHost. There are three kinds.

8
00:00:46,750 --> 00:00:52,973
A shared environment is a lightweight chat agent. Its tools run inside your own app.

9
00:00:53,850 --> 00:00:58,537
An isolated environment gives every session its own sandbox:

10
00:00:58,837 --> 00:01:05,153
a private workspace with a shell, the packages you choose, and network rules you control.

11
00:01:06,017 --> 00:01:12,560
A promotable session starts out cheap, then moves into its own sandbox the moment it needs one.

12
00:01:13,417 --> 00:01:19,066
Here is how the pieces fit together. Your app connects to a gateway that you run.

13
00:01:19,917 --> 00:01:26,902
The gateway signs you in with GitHub, and sends each session to a shared runtime or to its own sandbox.

14
00:01:27,202 --> 00:01:34,439
It keeps a durable record of every request your app must answer, so work can continue after your app goes away.

15
00:01:35,317 --> 00:01:39,791
Sandboxes reach the internet only through an egress proxy.

16
00:01:40,091 --> 00:01:47,788
It enforces your allowlists and adds secrets at the edge, so code in the sandbox only ever sees placeholders.

17
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:56,519
Model calls go through the gateway and use your own Copilot plan. Your GitHub token never enters a sandbox.

18
00:01:57,383 --> 00:02:01,796
Running it on your machine takes one command: aspire run.

19
00:02:02,096 --> 00:02:08,868
It starts the gateway, the sandbox images, the egress proxy, and both demos.

20
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:13,783
Every example in the design is also an automated check.

21
00:02:14,083 --> 00:02:21,273
The quickstart runs all thirteen against the real platform, with real models, and all thirteen pass.

22
00:02:22,150 --> 00:02:25,738
Demo one: an AI software factory.

23
00:02:26,617 --> 00:02:32,129
You describe the software you want, and a team of Copilot agents builds it.

24
00:02:32,429 --> 00:02:36,360
Here, we start the CSV ⇄ JSON toolkit.

25
00:02:37,217 --> 00:02:45,077
A planner agent splits the work into two tasks. It runs on a shared runtime, with no network access.

26
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:50,338
Each task goes to its own builder, in its own sandbox.

27
00:02:50,638 --> 00:02:55,907
Both builders work at the same time, writing code and running the tests.

28
00:02:56,783 --> 00:03:00,506
A reviewer on a different model checks each task.

29
00:03:00,806 --> 00:03:08,438
When it finds a problem, it sends the work back with the file and line, and the builder fixes it in the same sandbox.

30
00:03:09,317 --> 00:03:17,848
Finally, a release agent combines the tasks, adds integration tests and a README, and checks that everything passes.

31
00:03:18,717 --> 00:03:25,948
The result is a tested release you can download, and every station's files are kept as versioned artifacts.

32
00:03:26,817 --> 00:03:34,074
Any agent's session can also be opened in the Copilot CLI, attached to the same live conversation.

33
00:03:34,950 --> 00:03:38,103
Demo two: a personal assistant.

34
00:03:38,983 --> 00:03:45,687
This is an assistant you chat with, like a bot. It can read your mail and manage your calendar.

35
00:03:45,987 --> 00:03:52,529
Here it uses a clearly labeled demo inbox and calendar; you can connect your own Google account.

36
00:03:53,383 --> 00:04:02,272
Ask it to triage your inbox, and it sorts your messages into what needs a reply, what is just for your information, and what can wait.

37
00:04:03,150 --> 00:04:07,843
Ask it to book a meeting, and it checks your calendar for a free slot.

38
00:04:08,717 --> 00:04:15,625
But it always asks before it sends mail or changes your calendar. Here, we approve the invite.

39
00:04:16,483 --> 00:04:23,504
It also keeps working when you're not there. Ask for a reminder, and the platform schedules it.

40
00:04:24,383 --> 00:04:31,019
A minute later, the reminder runs on its own, as you, and the assistant sends you a message.

41
00:04:31,883 --> 00:04:36,957
Now we ask it to check the calendar in two minutes, and then close the tab.

42
00:04:37,817 --> 00:04:42,481
With no browser open, the platform still runs the request.

43
00:04:42,781 --> 00:04:48,524
When the agent needs the calendar, the platform calls the assistant's server directly.

44
00:04:49,383 --> 00:04:56,555
When we come back, the answer is waiting, and the calendar lookup is marked: handled while you were away.

45
00:04:57,417 --> 00:05:02,874
The same AppHost deploys to Azure with one more command: aspire deploy.

46
00:05:03,174 --> 00:05:08,837
The gateway, the demos, and the egress proxy run as Azure Container Apps.

47
00:05:09,137 --> 00:05:15,881
Each isolated session gets its own sandbox, and the platform's state is kept on Azure Files.

48
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:19,301
Both demos run there today.

49
00:05:20,183 --> 00:05:28,631
Eleven of the thirteen checks run against the deployment, and all eleven pass. The other two need a local webhook receiver.

50
00:05:29,483 --> 00:05:36,796
To recap: genuine Copilot SDK clients and sessions, running in environments you control,

51
00:05:37,096 --> 00:05:45,061
with sandboxes, network rules, secrets, offline work, schedules, and the Copilot CLI,

52
00:05:45,361 --> 00:05:50,036
all tested end to end with real models, locally and on Azure.

53
00:05:50,917 --> 00:05:58,255
To try it yourself: sign in with the GitHub CLI, install and build, and run aspire run.

54
00:05:58,555 --> 00:06:05,316
Then open the factory and the assistant. The README and the quickstart are the best places to start.

55
00:06:05,616 --> 00:06:07,634
Thanks for watching.
